WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.335
Injection happens when untrusted input changes the meaning of something the application sends to an interpreter or downstream system.

2
00:00:09.335 --> 00:00:18.102
That something might be a database query, command, document, template, script, URL, header, protocol message, or API request.

3
00:00:18.102 --> 00:00:22.161
The security failure is not simply that input was accepted.

4
00:00:22.161 --> 00:00:30.360
The failure is that data was allowed to become instructions or structure in a context where it should have remained data.

5
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.821
JavaScript and Node.

6
00:00:31.821 --> 00:00:35.068
js applications encounter many interpreters.

7
00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:45.459
A backend may build SQL queries, NoSQL queries, shell commands, template output, JSON documents, URLs, HTTP headers, and messages to downstream APIs.

8
00:00:45.459 --> 00:00:54.063
A frontend may render HTML, set attributes, build URLs, update the DOM, parse JSON, and interact with browser security rules.

9
00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:59.908
Each context has its own rules for what characters, types, structures, and APIs mean.

10
00:00:59.908 --> 00:01:03.723
This is why injection prevention is context-specific.

11
00:01:03.723 --> 00:01:09.000
Input validation checks whether data has the shape the application expects.

12
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.383
Parameterization keeps values separate from query structure.

13
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:17.929
Output encoding makes data safe for a specific rendering context.

14
00:01:17.929 --> 00:01:22.475
Escaping may be useful only when it matches the exact interpreter.

15
00:01:22.475 --> 00:01:27.021
Safe API design avoids string-built instructions when possible.

16
00:01:27.021 --> 00:01:31.323
These controls are related, but they are not interchangeable.

17
00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:38.142
A common mistake is trying to memorize dangerous inputs instead of understanding sensitive sinks.

18
00:01:38.142 --> 00:01:45.285
The safer mental model is to ask where untrusted data enters, where it flows, and where it is interpreted.

19
00:01:45.285 --> 00:01:57.299
If the data reaches a database, shell, template engine, browser, file path, URL, header, or external API, the developer must choose a control appropriate to that destination.

20
00:01:57.299 --> 00:02:02.251
The goal is simple to state and demanding to implement: keep data as data.

21
00:02:02.251 --> 00:02:15.402
JavaScript developers prevent injection by designing application boundaries so untrusted input cannot change command meaning, query structure, document structure, or executable logic.
